# Common laptop/desktop runtime additions. RavenGUI itself ships in the image.
#
# developer.packages duplicates every name in this file by hand, because
# raven-postinstall reads one .packages file and has no include. Add a package
# here and add it there too; developer.packages carries the one-line check
# that catches it when you do not.
linux-firmware
sof-firmware
alsa-ucm-conf
pipewire
pipewire-audio
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
bluez
bluez-utils
mesa-utils
vulkan-tools
vulkan-radeon
vulkan-intel

# The graphics stack huginn links against.
#
# The ISO stages these as bare .so files copied off the build host -- stage2's
# copy_libraries() and stage-gui.sh's stage_gui_libraries(). That is what makes
# the live image work, but `rvn owns` reports no package owns any of them, so
# on an installed system they were invisible to the package manager: never
# updated, never repaired, and no way to tell a corrupted one from a missing
# one. Declaring them here puts the same files under package management on any
# machine that asked for a desktop.
#
# These are the runtime half of ravengui's [dependencies] in
# packages/gui/ravengui/package.toml; keep the two in step.
#
# Two names from that list are deliberately absent. `libseat` is not a package
# on Arch -- the library ships inside seatd, so seatd covers both the daemon
# and the .so huginn links. `libudev` is not a package either; it comes from
# systemd-libs, which is already a base dependency. Listing either by name
# fails the whole profile, because raven-postinstall hands the entire list to
# one `rvn install`.
mesa
libdrm
libinput
libxkbcommon
wayland
seatd
xorg-xwayland

# The toolkit, for RavenFileManager.
#
# The image ships it -- stage-gui.sh builds it from javanhut/RavenFileManager
# alongside huginn and the terminal -- and, like the graphics stack above, it
# arrives as unowned files copied off a build host. It links a hundred and
# thirty-four shared libraries against huginn's seventeen, so the great
# majority of these come in as dependencies of gtk4 and libadwaita; they are
# named anyway, because a package that is installed as a dependency is one an
# unrelated removal can take with it.
#
# Four are here for reasons no linker reports:
#
#   glib2                      owns glib-compile-schemas. GSettings reads only
#                              the compiled gschemas.compiled, never the XML,
#                              and without it every GTK application aborts at
#                              startup on a schema lookup.
#   gsettings-desktop-schemas  libadwaita reads org.gnome.desktop.interface to
#                              choose light or dark; absent, it stays light.
#   shared-mime-info           without mime.cache every file reads as
#                              application/octet-stream.
#   bubblewrap                 since gdk-pixbuf 2.44 the image loaders live in
#                              glycin, which decodes inside a bwrap sandbox and
#                              refuses to decode without one. No bwrap, no
#                              images anywhere in the toolkit.
#
# Installing these is necessary and not sufficient: the caches above are
# derived state and nothing here rebuilds them. `raven-desktopinstall` is the
# script that installs this set *and* rebuilds them, and is the better command
# to reach for on an already-installed machine.
gtk4
libadwaita
glib2
gsettings-desktop-schemas
gsettings-system-schemas
shared-mime-info
desktop-file-utils
gtk-update-icon-cache
gdk-pixbuf2
librsvg
glycin
bubblewrap
dconf

# Two names the ISO already carries as unowned copied files.
#
# Same argument as the graphics stack above, applied to two things that have
# nothing to do with the desktop. stage2 copies /usr/bin/nft and
# /usr/bin/sysctl out of the build container, so the image has both binaries
# and `rvn owns` can account for neither: never updated, never repaired.
# Declaring them here puts them under package management on an installed
# machine, which for a firewall in particular is the difference between a
# security fix arriving with the next `rvn update` and not arriving.
#
#   nftables   what the nftables service in /etc/raven/init.toml runs against
#              /etc/nftables.conf. The service ships disabled, so this is the
#              package you want present before you decide to turn the
#              firewall on rather than while you are trying to. Installing it
#              will leave an /etc/nftables.conf.pacnew beside Raven's
#              ruleset: Arch's nftables owns that path too and rvn spares a
#              backup file it did not write, which is the correct outcome
#              here -- the shipped ruleset survives, and the upstream example
#              is there to diff against.
#   procps-ng  `sysctl`, and with it any way to ask whether
#              /usr/lib/sysctl.d/50-raven.conf actually took. ps, top, free
#              and uptime are from the same package and were already on the
#              image by accident of the build container carrying it.
nftables
procps-ng

# Optional in the sense that matters: nothing Raven ships needs any of these,
# and each one is here because software that is not Raven's does.
#
#   polkit   Raven has no polkitd. raven-rc, rvn, raven-powerd and
#            raven-firmware authorise by unix group on their own socket or by
#            already being root, and gain exactly nothing from an
#            authorization framework. What needs one is other people's
#            software: virt-manager, GNOME Disks, graphical updaters and
#            `fwupdmgr` all call CheckAuthorization before they will do
#            anything, and with nobody owning org.freedesktop.PolicyKit1 that
#            call fails rather than prompts. It is not a dialog you can
#            dismiss -- it is the tool not working, with an error that blames
#            D-Bus.
#
#            Installing this creates the polkitd account from the package's
#            own sysusers.d fragment (rvn applies those; systemd is not
#            involved) and activates the polkitd service template, which
#            ships DISABLED. `raven-rc enable polkitd` is the deliberate
#            second step, and /usr/share/raven/services/polkitd.toml is where
#            the whole tradeoff is written down -- including the setuid-root
#            /usr/bin/pkexec this package also brings, which is the honest
#            cost and the reason polkit is not in the base image.
#   flatpak  The answer to application availability. Raven's own repository
#            is small and the AUR path builds from source, unsigned, on the
#            machine you are sitting at -- neither is a way to get a current
#            browser, an IDE or a chat client this afternoon. Flatpak is, and
#            it does it without asking the base system to carry any of those
#            runtimes or their dependency graphs. The portal frontend the
#            sandboxed applications talk to is already shipped, as a user
#            service in configs/raven/user-services/xdg-desktop-portal.toml,
#            so the desktop side of this is in place. Adding a remote is not
#            done here: `flatpak remote-add --if-not-exists flathub ...` is a
#            decision about who may ship software to this machine, and that
#            belongs to the person who owns it.
#   sbctl    Secure Boot key management. RavenBoot is Raven's own EFI binary
#            and is signed by nobody Microsoft has heard of, so a machine
#            with Secure Boot enabled either boots Raven with the vendor keys
#            cleared or does not boot it at all. sbctl is what creates a key
#            set, enrols it while the firmware is in setup mode, and signs
#            \EFI\BOOT\BOOTX64.EFI and \EFI\raven\raven-boot.efi on the ESP.
#            Far easier to have installed before Secure Boot is turned on
#            than to obtain from a machine that will not boot.
polkit
flatpak
sbctl
